# MITRE ATT&CK T1059.001 — PowerShell

## ATT&CK Reference

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Tactic | Execution |
| Technique | Command and Scripting Interpreter |
| Sub-technique | PowerShell |
| Technique ID | T1059.001 |
| Lab Detection | DET-003 — Encoded PowerShell Execution |
| Primary Telemetry | Sysmon Event ID 1 |
| Supporting Telemetry | PowerShell Event ID 4104 |
| SIEM | Splunk Enterprise |
| Status | Validated |

Official MITRE ATT&CK reference:

[T1059.001 — PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001/)

---

# Technique Overview

PowerShell is a Windows command and scripting environment commonly used for legitimate system administration, automation, configuration, and management.

Because PowerShell provides extensive access to Windows functionality, it can also be used during adversary operations.

Security monitoring should therefore focus on execution behavior and context rather than treating PowerShell itself as malicious.

Potentially interesting characteristics include:

- Encoded commands
- Obfuscated scripts
- Non-interactive execution
- Hidden execution
- Unexpected parent processes
- Suspicious child processes
- Network activity
- Unusual script contents

Sprint 21 focuses specifically on:

```text
PowerShell
    +
Encoded Command Argument
```

---

