# Analyst Response Workflow

When DET-003 triggers:

```text
DET-003 ALERT
       |
       v
Identify Endpoint
       |
       v
Identify User
       |
       v
Review Full Command Line
       |
       v
Identify Parent Process
       |
       v
Review Script Block
       |
       v
Review Child Processes
       |
       v
Review Network Activity
       |
       v
Correlate Other Security Events
       |
       v
Determine Authorization
       |
       +-------------------------+
       |                         |
       v                         v
   Legitimate                Suspicious
       |                         |
       v                         v
 Document / Close          Investigate
                                 |
                                 v
                              Escalate
```

---

# Step 1 — Identify Endpoint

Determine:

```text
host
Computer
```

The analyst should establish:

- Which endpoint executed PowerShell
- Whether the endpoint is a workstation or server
- Whether the system is sensitive
- Whether PowerShell use is expected on that asset

---

# Step 2 — Identify User

Review:

```text
User
```

Determine:

- Which account executed PowerShell
- Whether the account is privileged
- Whether the user normally performs administrative activity
- Whether the account should be active on the endpoint

---

# Step 3 — Review Command Line

Review:

```text
CommandLine
```

Determine:

- Which PowerShell executable was used
- Which arguments were supplied
- Whether `-EncodedCommand` or an abbreviation was used
- Whether other unusual arguments were present

Examples of arguments that may deserve additional context include:

```text
-EncodedCommand
-enc
-NoProfile
-NonInteractive
-WindowStyle Hidden
```

No individual argument should automatically be considered malicious without context.

---

# Step 4 — Review Parent Process

Review:

```text
ParentImage
```

Determine how PowerShell was launched.

Process lineage:

```text
Parent Process
      |
      v
powershell.exe
      |
      v
Command
```

Unexpected process lineage may increase investigative priority.

---

# Step 5 — Review Script Block Logging

Search:

```spl
index=* EventCode=4104
```

Narrow by endpoint and time.

PowerShell Script Block Logging may reveal the actual content executed by PowerShell.

Correlation:

```text
Process Command Line
        +
Script Block Content
        |
        v
Better Understanding
of Execution
```

---

# Step 6 — Review Child Processes

Search Sysmon Event ID 1 around the detection time.

```spl
index=* EventCode=1
```

Determine whether PowerShell launched additional processes.

Potential chain:

```text
Parent
  |
  v
PowerShell
  |
  +---- Child Process
  |
  +---- Child Process
```

Unexpected follow-on execution may increase investigative significance.

---

# Step 7 — Review Network Activity

Search:

```spl
index=* EventCode=3
```

Narrow by:

```text
host
time
process
```

Determine whether PowerShell generated network connections.

Potential correlation:

```text
Encoded PowerShell
       +
External Network Connection
       |
       v
Higher Investigative Priority
```

The Sprint 21 validation payload was not expected to create suspicious network traffic.

---

# Step 8 — Correlate Other Detections

Review other SOC detections around the same endpoint and timestamp.

The lab currently provides examples such as:

```text
DET-001
Network Port Scan Detection

DET-002
Repeated Failed Authentication

DET-003
Encoded PowerShell Execution
```

Individual alerts provide useful signals.

Correlated alerts can provide stronger incident context.

---

# Step 9 — Determine Legitimate Use

Potential legitimate explanations include:

- Administrator automation
- Software deployment
- Configuration management
- IT support activity
- Installation scripts
- Security tooling
- Development workflows
- Authorized security testing

If expected:

```text
Validate Authorization
        |
        v
Document Findings
        |
        v
Close Alert
```

---

# Step 10 — Escalate Suspicious Activity

Potential escalation indicators include:

- Unknown user
- Privileged account involvement
- Unexpected endpoint
- Suspicious parent process
- Suspicious Script Block contents
- Unexpected child processes
- External network connections
- Additional endpoint detections
- Authentication anomalies
- Continued suspicious activity

Workflow:

```text
Suspicious PowerShell
        |
        v
Determine Scope
        |
        v
Correlate Telemetry
        |
        v
Identify Affected Assets
        |
        v
Escalate Incident
```

---

# Threat Hunting Workflow

Sprint 21 also introduced a broader PowerShell hunting search:

```spl
index=* "powershell"
("-EncodedCommand" OR "-NoProfile" OR "-NonInteractive" OR "Hidden")
```

Saved as:

```text
HUNT - Suspicious PowerShell Arguments
```

Hunting workflow:

```text
Broad PowerShell Search
        |
        v
Identify Interesting Arguments
        |
        v
Review User
        |
        v
Review Parent
        |
        v
Review Script
        |
        v
Review Network
        |
        v
Determine Context
```

---

# Detection vs. Threat Hunting

DET-003 provides a relatively narrow behavioral signal:

```text
EncodedCommand
      |
      v
DET-003
      |
      v
Alert
```

The hunting query intentionally provides broader visibility:

```text
EncodedCommand
NoProfile
NonInteractive
Hidden
      |
      v
Threat Hunt
      |
      v
Analyst Review
```

Separating detection from hunting prevents every interesting PowerShell characteristic from automatically becoming an alert.

---

# False Positive Analysis

PowerShell is heavily used for legitimate Windows administration.

Potential false positives include:

- System administration
- Software management
- Automation
- Configuration management
- Endpoint-management platforms
- Software installation
- Developer activity
- Security tooling
- Authorized testing

Therefore:

```text
PowerShell != Malicious
```

and:

```text
Encoded PowerShell != Confirmed Compromise
```

The analyst must evaluate execution context.

---

# Confidence Model

A simple future confidence model could use correlated behaviors.

### Lower Confidence

```text
Encoded PowerShell
```

### Increased Confidence

```text
Encoded PowerShell
        +
Unexpected Parent
```

### Higher Confidence

```text
Encoded PowerShell
        +
Unexpected Parent
        +
Suspicious Script Block
        +
Network Connection
        +
Unexpected Child Process
```

This demonstrates how SOC detections can evolve from single-event indicators into correlated behavioral analytics.

